# Enforce consistent line endings across platforms

# Default: auto-detect (respects .editorconfig / per-file overrides below)
* text=auto

# Rust source files — always LF (required by rustfmt newline_style = "Unix")
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.json5 text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Swift source files
*.swift text eol=lf

# C# / Windows project files — keep CRLF for Visual Studio compatibility
*.cs text eol=crlf
*.csproj text eol=crlf
*.sln text eol=crlf
*.appxmanifest text eol=crlf
*.xaml text eol=crlf

# Binary files — never touch line endings
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.svg binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.dll binary
*.dylib binary
*.so binary
*.a binary
*.xcframework binary

# MWBM-MEDIA-LANG policy copies must stay byte-for-byte identical to the
# master files in MWBMPartners/MeedyaSuite-core (the policy document says so
# itself, in docs/standards/media-language-bcp47-policy.md, section 8.3), and
# the copy checker (scripts/media-lang/check_copies.py) compares their exact
# bytes. Git can change a file's bytes when it checks the file out in four
# ways, and each line below switches all four off for one copy, overriding
# the generic rules above it in this file:
#
#   -text                   no line-ending conversion
#   -filter                 no filter program run over the file (a "clean"
#                           or "smudge" filter someone has set up)
#   -working-tree-encoding  no conversion into another text encoding
#   -ident                  no expansion of "$Id$" into a version string
#
# What can still override these lines (git takes each attribute from the
# setting with the highest priority):
#   * any LATER line in this same file that also matches the copy — git uses
#     the last matching line for each attribute, so it need not be a broader
#     one. Keep these lines at the end of the file;
#   * a .gitattributes closer to the copy: in the copy's own folder, or in a
#     folder between the copy and the top of the repository (a deeper file
#     wins over a shallower one). A .gitattributes in a folder that does not
#     contain the copy has no effect on it;
#   * .git/info/attributes on someone's own machine, which git always gives
#     the highest priority.
# The global and system attributes files have the LOWEST priority, so they
# do not override these lines. If anything does change a copy's bytes, the
# copy checker reports that copy as changed, which is the right answer: the
# bytes on that disk no longer match the master. (Proven in throwaway clones,
# using only each clone's own .git/config: a filter and an encoding set up
# there changed the copies with the old "-text"-only lines and left them
# byte for byte alone with these, including through the clone's own
# core.attributesFile; a later line in this file, a .gitattributes in
# docs/standards and a line in .git/info/attributes each still changed the
# policy document's line endings, and the copy checker reported it; a
# .gitattributes in help/, which does not hold a copy, changed nothing.)
docs/standards/media-language-bcp47-policy.md -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
docs/standards/MWBM-MEDIA-LANG.lock -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
tests/fixtures/bcp47-language-policy-v1.json -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
tests/fixtures/bcp47-language-policy-v1.schema.json -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
docs/standards/data/bcp47-language-data-v1.json -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
docs/standards/data/bcp47-language-data-v1.schema.json -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
scripts/media-lang/check_copies.py -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
